$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM (1s/r, 3 s/r)-1-(pentafluoroethyl)-3-(trimethylsilyl)cyclobutanol (7b). A
procedure similar to that for preparing 7a was used. A 100 mL, 3-necked,
round-bottomed flask was equipped with a gas inlet, a stopper, and a
septum. The flask was charged with 5.1 g (0.027 mol 1.28 equiv) of
TMS-CF2CF3, 3.0g (0.021 mol, 1 equiv) of 6, and 25 mL of THF. Under
argon, the stirred mixture was cooled to 0 °C in an ice-water bath,
stirred for 5 minutes, and TBAF (0.22 mL, 0.00022 mol, 1M in THF and 5%
w/w. H2O, 0.01 equiv) was then syringed into the mixture, whereupon
immediate gas evolution was noted and the solution became a dark
brownish-orange color. The ice bath was removed, and the mixture was
allowed to warm to room temperature, while stirring. After 3 hours, GC-MS
confirmed complete reaction of the starting material to the intermediate
silyl ether. In order to cleave the intermediate silyl ether, the flask
was placed in an ice-bath for five minutes, and 2.0 mL (0.11 mol, 5.24
equiv) of H2O and an additional 1.3 mL of TBAF solution (0.0013 mol, 1M
in THF and 5% w/w. H2O, 0.062 equiv) were added via syringe. The flask
was removed from the ice-bath and allowed to stir overnight, whereupon
GC-MS indicated complete conversion to 7b. Upon completion, approximately
1 gram of solid sodium chloride was added to the reaction mixture and it
was briefly stirred to assist separation of the THF from the water. Solid
NaCl remained in the bottom of the flask. The stirrer was shut off, and
the liquid was decanted into a separatory funnel, along with 75 mL of
ether, using some of the ether to rinse the salt left in the flask. The
mixture was then washed with brine ( 1 X 50 mL), and the two layers
separated. The brine layer was back-extracted with additional ether (3 X
25 mL), and these extracts were added to the first organic layer. The
combined organic layers were dried with Na2SO4 and filtered through
filter paper into a round bottom flask, using pentane to rinse the
Na2SO4. The solvent was removed in vacuo via rotary evaporation yielding
a thick orange-brown oil. The crude product was purified by vacuum
distillation (bp 80-85 °C @ 30 mm Hg) to give 7b as a white crystalline
solid. During vacuum distillation, the product was observed to solidify
in and clog the condenser, even when 45 °C water was circulated through
it using a submersible pump (as described for 7a). Consequently, it was
necessary to remove the water from the condenser (using air cooling
only), and heat it periodically with a heat gun during the distillation
to melt the solid down into the receiving cow. A total of 4.0 g of 7b was
obtained (73%). Ratio (1s, 3s) to (1r, 3r) 94:6 as confirmed by GC-MS and
integration of 19F NMR. mp 50-52 °C. For NMR, in cases where the minor
isomer was distinguishable, appropriate peaks are indicated. For GC-MS
two peaks were clearly observed in the chromatogram; only the MS for the
major isomer is reported. 1H NMR (CDCl3, 300 MHz) (1s, 3s) δ 0.014 (9H,
s), 1.43-1.56 (1H, m), 1.95-2.02 (2H, m), 2.10 (1H, s, OH), 2.69-2.77
(2H, m); (1r, 3r) δ -0.012 (9H, s, Me3Si), other peaks for the (1r, 3r)
isomer were too small to be seen or were obscured by the other isomer;
13C NMR (CDCl3, 75 MHz) (1s, 3s) δ -3.89 (CH3), 11.78 (CH), 31.45 (CH2, s
with fine splitting), 74.20 (C, t, J C-C-F = 27.2 Hz), 114.17 (CF2, t of
q, J C-F = 257 Hz, J C-C-F = 34.8 Hz), 119.61 (CF2, q of t, J C-F = 288
Hz, J C-C-F = 36.7 Hz); (1r, 3r) δ -3.78 (CH3), 14.27 (CH), 29.97 (CH2, s
with fine splitting), C, CF2 and CF3 too small to be seen; 19F NMR
(CDCl3, 282 MHz, TFE = -79.4 ppm) (1s, 3s) δ -131.19 (2F, s), -81.30 (3F,
s); (1r, 3r) δ -129.68 (2F, s), -81.86 (3F, s); GC-MS (EI) (1s, 3s) 262
([M]+, 0.012%), 247 ([M-CH3]+, 1.4%), 172, 4.6%) 133 (9.0%), 119 (8.2%),
109 (5.7%), 101 (27.9 %), 75 (45.7%), 73 (100%), 69 (11.9%), 59 (9.3%),
45 (17.8%), 43 (13.6%); FTIR (cm-1, neat, ATR) 3360 (m, br, OH), 2950
(m), 1350 (w), 1254 (m), 1211 (s), 1205 (vs), 1109 (s), 940 (m), 831 (s);
HRMS (EI), calcd for C8H12OF5Si [M-CH3]+ : 247.0578, found 247.0572.
